"Groups individual observations or ideas by meaningful affinity to discover themes without imposing a predefined hierarchy.\n\nUse when: Synthesize a substantial set of qualitative research notes. Make a team's emerging interpretation of evidence visible.\n\nAvoid when: A required classification system already determines where each item must belong.\n\nVisual grammar: Individual notes preserve one observation or idea. Spatial groups indicate emergent similarity. Theme labels summarize each group's shared meaning, with evidence references retained.\n\nBuild: Prepare concise notes that preserve their original evidence context. Cluster notes by observed relationship before naming the groups. Review outliers and competing interpretations, then write specific theme statements.\n\nCheck: Groups reflect evidence rather than only convenient topic labels. Original observations remain distinguishable from theme interpretations. Outliers are retained or explicitly explained.\n\nPitfalls: Moving notes to fit a desired conclusion. Using broad headings that erase important differences between experiences.\n\nExample: A visitor research team groups interview notes into arrival uncertainty, ticket confidence and navigation needs, retaining contradictory accounts.\n\nAlternatives: Mental model diagram, Experience map."
